    Q1 [label="Reward depends on\nside information?",
        shape=diamond, fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Q2 [label="Action changes\nwhat you see next?",
        shape=diamond, fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    MAB  [label="K-armed bandit\nUCB1, Thompson",
          fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    CB   [label="Contextual bandit\nLinUCB, Lin-TS",
          fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    RL   [label="Reinforcement learning\nQ-learning, policy search",
          fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];

    Q1 -> MAB [label="  no"];
    Q1 -> Q2  [label="  yes"];
    Q2 -> CB  [label="  no"];
    Q2 -> RL  [label="  yes"];
}
```

- @Examples@
  - _Bandit_: which of 5 checkout button colors converts best
  - _Contextual bandit_: which article to show _this_ reader now
  - _RL_: which lesson to teach a student next, where the choice changes what
    the student knows tomorrow

# Practical Issues

* Delayed Feedback

- @Problem@: bandit theory assumes $R_t$ arrives before round $t+1$, but rewards
  in the real world lag
  - E.g., a click arrives in seconds, a conversion in days, a churn signal in
    months
  - Meanwhile the algorithm must keep serving traffic on stale statistics

- @Properties@: with a delay of $d$ rounds
  - Up to $d$ decisions are made with no feedback, so the algorithm behaves as
    if it had a smaller effective sample
    - For stochastic bandits, regret grows additively by $O(K d)$
    - For adversarial bandits, regret grows multiplicatively by $\sqrt{d}$

- @Techniques@
  - _Batching_: update once per batch, accept the delay explicitly
    - Thompson Sampling degrades gracefully, since it randomizes within a batch
    - UCB repeats the same arm for the whole batch, wasting it
  - _Optimistic imputation_: assume a pending reward is 0 (for conversions this
    is usually the truth), and correct on arrival
  - _Attribution window_: define a fixed horizon after which a non-conversion is
    a 0, so rewards stop being pending

- @Remark@: reward that is _fast and correlated_ is better than the one that is
  _slow and exact_

* Offline Evaluation

- @Problem@: we cannot A/B test every candidate bandit policy in production
  - Need to estimate the value of a new policy $\pi$ from logs generated by an
    old policy $\pi_0$

- @Key idea@: this is **off-policy evaluation**, the same problem as causal
  inference from observational data
  - The logging policy is the "treatment assignment mechanism"

- @Techniques@
  - _Replay / rejection sampling_ (Li et al., 2011): if $\pi_0$ was uniform,
    step through the log and keep only the records where $\pi$ picked the same
    arm as was logged
    - Unbiased, trivially simple, but discards a fraction $1 - 1/K$ of the data
  - _Inverse propensity scoring (IPS)_: weight each logged reward by
    $\pi(a_t|x_t) / \pi_0(a_t|x_t)$
    - Unbiased, but high variance when the two policies disagree
  - _Doubly robust_: combine IPS with a learned reward model
    - Unbiased if _either_ the propensities or the reward model is correct

- @Assumptions@: all of these need **logged propensities**
  - $\pi_0(a|x) > 0$ for every arm you want to evaluate
  - _Practical rule_: log the propensity at serving time, always
  - A deterministic logging policy makes offline evaluation impossible

* Safety, Fairness, and Other Constraints

- @Problem@: minimizing regret is rarely the only objective in production

- @Challenges@
  - _Safety_: a bandit is allowed to explore a catastrophic arm exactly once,
    which may be once too many
  - _Guardrails_: revenue may improve while latency or complaints degrade
  - _Fairness_: arms may represent sellers or creators, so allocation is a
    resource-distribution decision, not only an optimization
  - _Interference_: in a marketplace, showing an item to one user removes it for
    another, breaking the independence assumption

- @Techniques@
  - _Conservative bandits_: constrain cumulative reward to stay within a
    fraction of a known baseline arm at all times
  - _Constrained / knapsack bandits_: maximize reward subject to budget or
    resource constraints
  - _Allocation floors_: force $p_{t,i} \ge \eta$ for every arm
    - Costs a little regret, buys fairness and unbiased logs at once
  - _Human gating_: require approval before a new arm enters the pool

- @Remark@: most production incidents come from these constraints, not from
  picking the wrong index formula
